XR presentation: the frame into AHardwareBuffers for an OpenXR layer (Android)

WakerQuest (android/xr/wq_xr.cpp) shows the game on a screen in the Meta Quest through OpenXR, on its
own thread and GLES context. While it has enabled it (aurora_xr_present_enable, include/aurora/xr.h),
present_frame draws the EFB copy and ImGui into one of three AHardwareBuffers (R8G8B8A8_UNORM, a 4:3
target as tall as the present source) imported with SharedTextureMemoryAHardwareBuffer, instead of
acquiring and presenting the window surface; begin_frame no longer needs a presentable window then.
After the submit, EndAccess's SyncFD fence goes with the buffer to the XR layer, which takes the newest
frame (aurora_xr_present_take) and reports when its GPU has read it (aurora_xr_present_done): the
render worker never writes the published frame nor the one being read. The device asks for the
SharedTextureMemoryAHardwareBuffer and SharedFenceSyncFD features on Android when the adapter has
them; without them, or when anything fails, frames go to the surface as before. Elsewhere nothing
changes.

